-- title:
--   Proposition 3.1 — the regulator map φᵃ_B: unique càdlàg solution of (3.1), Lipschitz on bounded intervals, measurable (with B({0}) < 1)
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a cumulative distribution function on $\mathbb R$, with law $\mu$, such that $B$ does not put all its mass at $0$:
--   $$\mu(\{0\})<1 .$$
--   Let $a\in\mathbb R$. Then there is a map $\varphi^a_B:D[0,\infty)\to D[0,\infty)$ such that
--
--   1. for every $x\in D[0,\infty)$, $z=\varphi^a_B(x)$ solves (3.1):
--   $$z(t)=x(t)+\int_{[0,t]}(z(t-s)+a)^+\,dB(s),\qquad t\ge0;$$
--   2. every $z\in D[0,\infty)$ solving (3.1) with input $x$ equals $\varphi^a_B(x)$ on $[0,\infty)$;
--   3. for every $T\ge0$ there is a constant $C_T$ with
--   $$\|\varphi^a_B(x_1)-\varphi^a_B(x_2)\|_T\le C_T\,\|x_1-x_2\|_T\qquad\text{for all } x_1,x_2\in D[0,\infty);$$
--   4. $\varphi^a_B$ is measurable for the Kolmogorov σ-algebra of $D[0,\infty)$ (on both sides).
--
--   This is the regulator map on which the paper's fluid and diffusion limits (Theorems 4.1 and 5.1) are built: the fluid-scaled queue length is $\varphi^{-1}_F$ of a free process and the diffusion-scaled one is $\varphi^{0}_F$ of another, and those proofs combine the continuity and measurability of the map with the continuous mapping theorem.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The hypothesis $\mu(\{0\})<1$ is added: the paper states the proposition for every distribution function, but for $B=\mathbf 1\{\cdot\ge0\}$, $a=0$ and $x\equiv1$ equation (3.1) reads $z(t)=1+z(t)^+$, which has no solution. The paper's proof treats a point mass at $c>0$ and the condition $B(y+\delta)-B(y)<\varepsilon<1$, and the case $c=0$ falls between them. Every service law of mean $1$ on $[0,\infty)$, the paper's only use, satisfies $\mu(\{0\})<1$. The paper's phrases are made explicit as follows: "unique solution in $D[0,\infty)$" is uniqueness among càdlàg paths up to equality on $[0,\infty)$; "Lipschitz continuous in the topology of uniform convergence over bounded intervals" is item 3, with $C_T$ depending only on $T$ (and $B$, $a$), not on $x_1,x_2$; "measurable with respect to the Borel σ-field generated by the Skorohod $J_1$ topology" is measurability for the σ-algebra generated by the evaluations $x\mapsto x(t)$, $t\ge0$, which the paper identifies with the $J_1$ Borel σ-field on p. 35 (Billingsley 1999, Theorem 16.6). The integral is over the closed interval $[0,t]$, an atom at $0$ included.
-- source:
--   Reed, The G/GI/N Queue in the Halfin–Whitt Regime, arXiv:0912.2837v1, p. 9, Proposition 3.1 (proof pp. 32–36)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ReedGGN_Regulator_PathSpace
import Definitions.Def_ReedGGN_Regulator_Equation

namespace ReedGGN.Regulator

open MeasureTheory

/-- Proposition 3.1 (p. 9), with the added hypothesis `B({0}) < 1` (`μ {0} < 1`), without which
the statement is false (`B` = unit mass at `0`, `a = 0`, `x ≡ 1` has no solution).
There is a map `φ^a_B : D[0,∞) → D[0,∞)` such that `φ^a_B(x)` solves (3.1), every càdlàg
solution agrees with it on `[0, ∞)`, it is Lipschitz in the supremum metric on every
`[0, T]` (constant depending on `T` only), and it is measurable for the Kolmogorov
σ-algebra (= the Borel σ-algebra of the Skorohod `J₁` topology). -/
theorem proposition_3_1 (μ : Measure ℝ) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] (hμ0 : μ {0} < 1) (a : ℝ) :
    ∃ Φ : Dpath → Dpath,
      (∀ x : Dpath, SolvesRegulator μ a x.path (Φ x).path) ∧
      (∀ (x z : Dpath), SolvesRegulator μ a x.path z.path →
        Set.EqOn z.path (Φ x).path (Set.Ici 0)) ∧
      (∀ T : ℝ, 0 ≤ T → ∃ C : ℝ, ∀ x₁ x₂ : Dpath,
        supNorm T ((Φ x₁).path - (Φ x₂).path) ≤ C * supNorm T (x₁.path - x₂.path)) ∧
      Measurable Φ := by sorry

end ReedGGN.Regulator
